  "gdTitle": "Field guide",
  "gdToolSub": "Mesh basics, batteries in the cold, solar and antennas",
  "gdSecMesh": "Getting on the mesh",
  "gdMesh1": "Your phone talks to your radio over Bluetooth, USB or Wi-Fi; the radio talks to the mesh over LoRa. Messages still work with no phone signal.",
  "gdMesh2": "Every radio that hears a message can pass it on. Each pass is a hop, and the hop limit (3 by default) sets how far a message spreads.",
  "gdMesh3": "Leave your role as Client unless your radio sits somewhere high and is always on. Too many routers make the mesh worse, not better.",
  "gdMesh4": "Keep messages short and avoid flooding busy channels: every message uses shared airtime.",
  "gdSecCold": "Batteries in the cold",
  "gdCold1": "Don’t charge lithium batteries below freezing (0 °C / 32 °F): it damages them permanently.",
  "gdCold2": "Carry the radio inside your jacket. Cold cuts capacity, but it comes back once the battery warms up.",
  "gdCold3": "For fixed sites in cold climates, LiFePO4 copes better than lithium-ion; a heated or insulated box helps either.",
  "gdSecSolar": "Solar for fixed nodes",
  "gdSolar1": "A node uses little power, but size the panel and battery for your darkest winter week, not a summer day.",
  "gdSolar2": "Face the panel toward the equator and tilt it steeply in winter; keep snow and shade off it.",
  "gdSecAntenna": "Antennas and placement",
  "gdAnt1": "Height beats power: a radio a few meters up reaches far more than one on a desk.",
  "gdAnt2": "Keep the antenna vertical and clear of metal, and use one made for your band (for example 915 MHz in the US, 868 MHz in Europe). Never transmit without one.",
  "gdSecMore": "Learn more",
